rt":793,"end":803},{"pmid":"8106150","text":"Baclofen","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D001418","start":815,"end":823},{"pmid":"8106150","text":"dystonic","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D020821","start":869,"end":877},{"pmid":"8106150","text":"dyskinesia","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D004409","start":886,"end":896},{"pmid":"8106150","text":"Atropine","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D001285","start":898,"end":906},{"pmid":"8106150","text":"dystonic","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D020821","start":921,"end":929},{"pmid":"8106150","text":"chorea","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D002819","start":945,"end":951}],"gold_relations":"L-DOPA/benserazide | CID | dyskinesia\nMPTP | CID | parkinsonian","metrics":{"precision":0.0,"recall":0.0,"f1":0.0,"tp":0,"fp":14,"fn":2,"rel_precision":0.0,"rel_recall":0.0,"rel_f1":0.0,"rel_tp":0,"rel_fp":14,"rel_fn":2},"pmid":"8106150","relations":["D001418 | CID | D004409","D001285 | CID | D004421","D001418 | CID | D004421","D015632 | CID | D010300","D008614 | CID | D002547","D003000 | CID | D002547","D016291 | CID | D002547","D008784 | CID | D002547","D011433 | CID | D002547","D007980 | CID | D004409","D015016 | CID | D002547","D001285 | CID | D002819","D011433 | CID | D010300","D010830 | CID | D002547"],"text":"Effect of nondopaminergic drugs on L-dopa-induced dyskinesias in MPTP-treated monkeys. A group of four monkeys was rendered parkinsonian with the toxin MPTP. They were then treated chronically with L-DOPA/benserazide 50/12.5 mg/kg given orally daily for 2 months. This dose produced a striking antiparkinsonian effect, but all animals manifested dyskinesia. A series of agents acting primarily on neurotransmitters other than dopamine were then tested in combination with L-DOPA to see if the dyskinetic movements would be modified. Several drugs, including clonidine, physostigmine, methysergide, 5-MDOT, propranolol, and MK-801, markedly reduced the dyskinetic movements but at the cost of a return of parkinsonian symptomatology. However, yohimbine and meperidine reduced predominantly the dyskinetic movements. Baclofen was also useful in one monkey against a more dystonic form of dyskinesia. Atropine converted the dystonic movements into chorea."}